  "raw": {
    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530380/open-rank-tenure-track-faculty-position-nuclear-engineering": {
      "title": "Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position - Nuclear Engineering",
      "description": "Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position - Nuclear Engineering Apply now Job no: 530380 Position type: Faculty Full Time Benefit Status: Benefited-Union Campus: UMass Lowell Department: Chemical Engineering Applications Open: Oct 14 2026 Applications Close: Open until filled General Summary of Position: The University of Massachusetts Lowell (UMass Lowell) invites applications for a full-time, open-rank, tenure-track/tenured faculty position in Nuclear Engineering beginning in September 2027. We seek collaborative scholars pursuing innovative, high-impact research in nuclear reactor engineering, particularly the development of advanced computational, experimental, data-driven, and AI/ML-enabled methods for reactor analysis, design, safety, and operation. Areas of interest include nuclear thermal-hydraulics and heat transfer; reactor physics; multiphysics and multiscale modeling and simulation; reactor safety; and reactor design and analysis. Applications to advanced reactor systems (fission or fusion) and emerging nuclear energy technologies are particularly encouraged. Emerging research directions involving high-fidelity and reduced-order modeling, uncertainty quantification, integration of experiments and modeling, digital engineering, advanced sensing and controls, and physics-informed hybrid modeling methods are also of interest. The successful candidate will be expected to establish and sustain an externally funded research program, advise undergraduate and graduate students, and contribute to collaborative research across the university. A strong commitment to teaching and mentoring is essential, including contributions to core and elective Nuclear Engineering courses, development of courses in the candidate’s area of expertise, and fostering a diverse and inclusive academic community. Department of Chemical Engineering and Nuclear Engineering Program UMass Lowell has a broad and growing program in nuclear science and engineering. The Department of Chemical Engineering is home to the ABET-accredited Bachelor of Science in Chemical Engineering with a Nuclear Engineering Option, while interdisciplinary MS and PhD programs in Nuclear (Energy) Engineering bring together faculty and resources from across the Francis College of Engineering and Kennedy College of Sciences. Complementary programs include Nuclear Physics, Radiological Sciences, and Medical Physics. The Department of Chemical Engineering values interdisciplinary collaboration, academic excellence, innovation, and student success. Faculty engage in collaborative research across the university and with national laboratories, industry, and other academic institutions, providing opportunities for interdisciplinary research and education. UMass Lowell’s Radiation Laboratory provides substantial nuclear research infrastructure, including a 1-MW research reactor, 5.5-MV Van de Graaff accelerator, and radiochemistry and radiation measurement facilities. Candidates whose research intersects with these capabilities will have opportunities to incorporate them into their research and educational activities. The University of Massachusetts Lowell The University of Massachusetts Lowell is a nationally recognized Carnegie R1 research university located 25 miles northwest of Boston in Massachusetts’ high-tech corridor. With more than 17,000 students and over $120 million in sponsored research annually, UMass Lowell provides a dynamic environment for research, education, and interdisciplinary collaboration. The Wall Street Journal ranks UMass Lowell as the No. 1 public university in Massachusetts and the top public institution in the state for social mobility. The university is committed to research excellence, student success, community partnership, and a positive and inclusive campus culture. Minimum Qualifications (Required): A PhD or equivalent degree in Nuclear Engineering or a closely related field by the time of appointment. A strong record of research appropriate to the candidate’s career stage and demonstrated potential to establish and sustain an externally funded research program. Demonstrated potential for excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching and student mentorship. Interest in collaborative and interdisciplinary research. Appointment at the Associate Professor or Professor level will require a strong record of research, teaching, mentorship, and professional accomplishment commensurate with appointment at that rank. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is full-time, benefited, and represented by the Massachusetts Society of Professors/MTA/NEA. The anticipated salary range, pursuant to the applicable Collective Bargaining Agreement, lists minimum salaries of $75,000 for Assistant Professor, $85,000 for Associate Professor, and $100,000 for Professor. We also anticipate an actual minimum salary no less than $115,000. The anticipated maximum salary, depending on rank, qualifications, and departmental considerations, is not expected to exceed $205,000 for full Professor rank). Review of applications will begin on November 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Please include the following required documents with your application: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Cover Letter Teaching Statement/Philosophy Research Statement Names and contact information for three professional references Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530383/faculty-parttime-finance": {
      "title": "Faculty Part-Time - Finance",
      "description": "Faculty Part-Time - Finance Apply now Job no: 530383 Position type: Faculty Part Time/Adjunct Campus: UMass Lowell Applications Open: Oct 13 2026 Applications Close: Open until filled General Summary of the Position: The Manning School of Business at the University of Massachusetts Lowell invites applications for Adjunct Professors (standard teaching load 1-2 courses per semester). The successful candidates will be expected to teach a variety of finance classes to our undergraduate students starting January 2027. One immediate need is FINA.2000 Personal Finance. Minimum Qualifications (Required): Applicants must have earned a graduate degree in Finance in an AACSB-accredited school Ability or experience teaching finance classes Industry or professional work experience with continued professional engagement Excellent interpersonal and communication skills Ability to work effectively in teams Experience using Canvas or similar learning management systems Preferred Qualifications: At least ten years of industry or professional work experience Professional experience should reflect substantial level of responsibility in a business or other enterprise operated in accord with business management principles Relevant finance certifications (e.g. Certified Financial Planner (CFP)) Special Instructions to Applicants: Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. However, the position may close when an adequate number of qualified applications is received. This is a part-time, non-benefited, Union of Adjunct Faculty, Local 1596, UAW position. For Adjunct Faculty salaries, please refer to the Adjunct Faculty salary chart for the rates. Please include a CV and cover letter with your application. Names and contact information of three references will be required during the application process. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
